Subject: [PATCH] skbq: validate the cached vif before completing a Tx SKB

A Tx SKB can outlive the interface it was queued for. Frames sit in
morse_skbq::skbq or morse_skbq::pending until the chip reports a Tx status,
which may take up to tx_status_lifetime_ms (15 s by default), and neither
.remove_interface nor .stop nor .flush drops those frames or flushes the
workqueue that completes them. Meanwhile the completion path takes the vif
straight out of ieee80211_tx_info::control.vif, a raw pointer cached by
mac80211 when the frame was queued, and dereferences it without any
validity or NULL check. Once the interface is gone that pointer is dangling,
and the frames still queued for it are completed against freed memory.

Reconfiguring an interface is enough to hit this: the vaps are torn down and
rebuilt in a few hundred milliseconds, far inside the status lifetime. Two
crashes were seen in the same function, 0x3c apart, both consuming the same
cached pointer: one in ieee80211_find_sta(), which reaches the mac80211
sub_if_data 3040 bytes below the vif, and one in morse_bss_stats_update_tx(),
which reaches morse_vif::ap 1568 bytes above it. Both ran with mq->lock held,
so the oops happens in interrupt context and the box panics instead of just
losing the worker. A slow or timing-out bus makes the window wider by leaving
frames pending for longer.

Resolve the vif through a new helper instead. The driver's own vif table is
the only source of truth: the cached pointer is accepted only while that same
pointer value is still registered in the table, and it is compared, never
dereferenced. When the SKB carries no cached pointer the helper falls back to
the recorded interface id exactly as the old code did, so frames that do carry
a cached pointer no longer depend on the firmware echoing an id back. Every
consumer of the resolved vif now handles NULL by completing or dropping the
frame without touching per-interface state, and each one holds the RCU read
lock from the lookup through to its last dereference.

That is sound because the interface storage is released by free_netdev() from
netdev_run_todo(), and every path leading there first passes the
synchronize_net() that unregister_netdevice() runs once all interfaces have
been closed. The table entry is dropped on every path that leads to the
storage being released: .remove_interface clears it for an interface that came
up, and .add_interface now clears it on its own failure path too, because
mac80211 does not call .remove_interface for an interface whose .add_interface
returned an error while its netdev is still freed. Without that second half
the check would compare a dangling cached pointer against an equally dangling
table entry and accept it, so it is a precondition of the validation rather
than an extra hardening step. Only the table entry is dropped there; the
per-vif resources that a partly completed add leaked are a separate issue.
The grace period that precedes the release therefore always begins after the
entry has been dropped, whether the interface was brought down before being
deleted, deleted while still up, or never came up at all. This relies on every
interface type the driver advertises being backed by a netdev, which holds.

Known limitations. Comparing pointer values cannot distinguish a freed vif
from a new one that happens to be allocated at the same address, so in that
case the frame's statistics are attributed to the new BSS; the same applies to
a reused interface id on the fallback path. The result is misattribution, not
a crash. The vif table is also briefly empty while a hardware restart
reconfigures, and while mac80211 removes and re-adds a running interface to
change its MAC address; Tx statuses arriving in those windows are dropped
conservatively. The copy of the status fill path built when the rate control
module is disabled is fixed the same way for symmetry, though it is not
compiled in this configuration.

Upstream carries the same unsafe code. The vif resolution and all three
dereferences are identical in 1.17.8, mm6108-2.0.1, mm6108-2.1.1 and in
mm8108-2.1.0, which is the current main branch, so there is nothing to
backport and this fix is written locally. Re-evaluate it when the driver is
next uprevved.

--- a/skbq.h
+++ b/skbq.h
@@ -109,2 +109,52 @@
 /**
+ * morse_skbq_tx_vif_get() - Resolve the still-live vif a queued Tx SKB belongs to.
+ *
+ * @mors:   Morse device.
+ * @skb:    The Tx SKB being completed or dropped. Only its control buffer is read.
+ * @vif_id: Interface id recorded for this frame, taken either from the Morse Tx
+ *          header (morse_buff_skb_header::tx_info.flags) or from the Tx status
+ *          reported by the chip (morse_skb_tx_status::flags). It is only used when
+ *          the SKB has no cached vif pointer, which matches what the driver did
+ *          before this helper existed.
+ *
+ * A Tx SKB can outlive the struct ieee80211_vif it was queued for: it may sit in
+ * morse_skbq::skbq or morse_skbq::pending for seconds (see tx_status_lifetime_ms)
+ * while the interface is torn down. ieee80211_tx_info::control.vif is a raw pointer
+ * cached when the frame was queued and must never be dereferenced on its own after
+ * that point. This helper accepts the cached pointer only while the same pointer
+ * value is still registered in the driver's vif table, and otherwise falls back to
+ * @vif_id.
+ *
+ * Must be called from an RCU read-side critical section, and the returned vif (and
+ * anything reached through it, including its drv_priv) may only be dereferenced
+ * inside that same section. The table entry is dropped on every path that leads to
+ * the interface storage being released: .remove_interface clears it for interfaces
+ * that came up, and .add_interface clears it on its own failure path, because
+ * mac80211 does not call .remove_interface for an interface whose .add_interface
+ * returned an error. The storage itself is only released by free_netdev() from
+ * netdev_run_todo(), which is always reached after the synchronize_net() that
+ * unregister_netdevice() runs once every interface has already been closed. So the
+ * grace period that precedes the release always begins after the table entry has
+ * been dropped, and an RCU section that observed a live table entry keeps the
+ * storage alive until it ends. This holds whether the interface is brought down
+ * before it is deleted, deleted while still up, or never came up at all. It relies
+ * on every supported interface type being backed by a netdev, which is true for all
+ * types this driver advertises.
+ *
+ * Return: the live vif, or NULL. NULL is a normal, expected outcome and callers
+ *         must handle it by completing or dropping the frame without touching any
+ *         per-interface state. Its causes are:
+ *          - the interface was removed while the frame was in flight;
+ *          - adding the interface failed part way through, so the driver dropped the
+ *            table entry again and the interface will never be usable;
+ *          - the driver is between a hardware restart's interface cleanup and the
+ *            mac80211 reconfiguration that re-adds the interfaces, during which the
+ *            table is empty even though the mac80211 interfaces still exist (the
+ *            same is true for the short window in which mac80211 re-adds an
+ *            interface to change its MAC address while it is running).
+ */
+struct ieee80211_vif *morse_skbq_tx_vif_get(struct morse *mors, struct sk_buff *skb,
+					    int vif_id);
+
+/**
  * @brief Flush pending and in-flight tx SKBs from the queue.
--- a/skbq.c
+++ b/skbq.c
@@ -108,4 +108,5 @@
 	rcu_read_lock();
-	vif = txi->control.vif ? txi->control.vif : morse_get_vif_from_tx_status(mors, tx_sts);
-	sta = ieee80211_find_sta(vif, hdr->addr1);
+	vif = morse_skbq_tx_vif_get(mors, skb,
+				    MORSE_TX_CONF_FLAGS_VIF_ID_GET(le32_to_cpu(tx_sts->flags)));
+	sta = vif ? ieee80211_find_sta(vif, hdr->addr1) : NULL;
 	if (sta)
@@ -398,2 +399,32 @@

+/* See the kerneldoc for this function in skbq.h. */
+struct ieee80211_vif *morse_skbq_tx_vif_get(struct morse *mors, struct sk_buff *skb,
+					    int vif_id)
+{
+	struct ieee80211_vif *cached = IEEE80211_SKB_CB(skb)->control.vif;
+	struct ieee80211_vif *vif = NULL;
+	int i;
+
+	/* No cached pointer: resolve from the id recorded for this frame. This is
+	 * the same fallback the driver used before this helper existed, and it is
+	 * already range checked and locked internally.
+	 */
+	if (!cached)
+		return morse_get_vif_from_vif_id(mors, vif_id);
+
+	/* Pointer comparison only - the cached value is never dereferenced. If it is
+	 * no longer registered, the frame has outlived its interface.
+	 */
+	spin_lock_bh(&mors->vif_list_lock);
+	for (i = 0; i < mors->max_vifs; i++) {
+		if (__morse_get_vif_from_vif_id(mors, i) == cached) {
+			vif = cached;
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+	spin_unlock_bh(&mors->vif_list_lock);
+
+	return vif;
+}
+
 static void morse_skbq_sta_eosp(struct morse *mors, struct sk_buff *skb)
@@ -404,5 +435,8 @@

-	vif = (txi->control.vif) ? txi->control.vif :
-	    morse_get_vif_from_vif_id(mors, MORSE_TX_CONF_FLAGS_VIF_ID_GET
-				      (le32_to_cpu(hdr->tx_info.flags)));
+	/* Held from resolving the vif through to the last dereference of it. */
+	rcu_read_lock();
+	vif = morse_skbq_tx_vif_get(mors, skb,
+				    MORSE_TX_CONF_FLAGS_VIF_ID_GET(le32_to_cpu(hdr->tx_info.flags)));
+
+	/* The vif id above must be read before the Morse header is pulled off. */
 	morse_skb_remove_hdr_after_sent_to_chip(skb);
@@ -413,3 +447,3 @@
 	if (txi->flags & IEEE80211_TX_STATUS_EOSP) {
-		struct ieee80211_hdr *hdr = (struct ieee80211_hdr *)skb->data;
+		struct ieee80211_hdr *hdr80211 = (struct ieee80211_hdr *)skb->data;
 		struct ieee80211_sta *sta;
@@ -417,10 +451,8 @@
 		if (vif) {
-			/* Must be held while finding and dereferencing sta */
-			rcu_read_lock();
-			sta = ieee80211_find_sta(vif, hdr->addr1);
+			sta = ieee80211_find_sta(vif, hdr80211->addr1);
 			if (sta)
 				ieee80211_sta_eosp(sta);
-			rcu_read_unlock();
-		}
-	}
+		}
+	}
+	rcu_read_unlock();
 }
@@ -455,5 +487,6 @@
 {
-	struct ieee80211_tx_info *txi = IEEE80211_SKB_CB(skb);
 	struct ieee80211_vif *vif = NULL;
 	struct morse_vif *mors_vif;
+	bool can_filter;
+	bool ap_like;

@@ -464,11 +497,18 @@
 		mors_vif = morse_wiphy_get_sta_vif(mq->mors);
+		can_filter = mors_vif && mors_vif->supports_ps_filter;
+		ap_like = false;
 	} else {
-		vif = txi->control.vif ?
-		    txi->control.vif : morse_get_vif_from_tx_status(mq->mors, tx_sts);
-		mors_vif = ieee80211_vif_to_morse_vif(vif);
-	}
-
-	if (!mors_vif->supports_ps_filter) {
-		/* Do not rebuffer invalid pages, or on VIFs that do not support
-		 * PS filtering.
+		/* Held from resolving the vif through to the last dereference. */
+		rcu_read_lock();
+		vif = morse_skbq_tx_vif_get(mq->mors, skb,
+				MORSE_TX_CONF_FLAGS_VIF_ID_GET(le32_to_cpu(tx_sts->flags)));
+		mors_vif = vif ? ieee80211_vif_to_morse_vif(vif) : NULL;
+		can_filter = mors_vif && mors_vif->supports_ps_filter;
+		ap_like = vif && vif->type != NL80211_IFTYPE_STATION;
+		rcu_read_unlock();
+	}
+
+	if (!can_filter) {
+		/* Do not rebuffer invalid pages, frames whose interface has gone
+		 * away, or VIFs that do not support PS filtering.
 		 */
@@ -479,3 +519,3 @@
 	/* mac80211 handles per-station re-buffering in AP mode */
-	if (vif && vif->type != NL80211_IFTYPE_STATION)
+	if (ap_like)
 		return false;
@@ -533,2 +573,29 @@
 			continue;
+		}
+
+		/* Only softmac, non-monitor completions resolve a vif below. */
+		if (!is_fullmac_mode() && !mors->monitor_mode) {
+			bool vif_gone;
+
+			rcu_read_lock();
+			vif_gone = !morse_skbq_tx_vif_get(mors, tx_skb,
+					MORSE_TX_CONF_FLAGS_VIF_ID_GET(
+						le32_to_cpu(tx_sts->flags)));
+			rcu_read_unlock();
+
+			if (vif_gone) {
+				/* No live interface owns this frame any more: it was
+				 * either removed while the frame was in flight, or
+				 * the vif table is empty because a hardware restart
+				 * has not finished reconfiguring. Drop the frame
+				 * instead of reporting a status against an interface
+				 * we cannot resolve.
+				 */
+				MORSE_SKB_ERR_RATELIMITED(mors,
+					"tx status with no live interface [pktid:%u chan:%u]\n",
+					le32_to_cpu(tx_sts->pkt_id), tx_sts->channel);
+				__skbq_drop_pending_skb(mq, tx_skb);
+				spin_unlock_bh(&mq->lock);
+				continue;
+			}
 		}
@@ -1346,4 +1413,17 @@

-		vif = txi->control.vif ? txi->control.vif :
-				morse_get_vif_from_tx_status(mors, tx_sts);
+		vif = morse_skbq_tx_vif_get(mors, skb,
+				MORSE_TX_CONF_FLAGS_VIF_ID_GET(le32_to_cpu(tx_sts->flags)));
+		if (!vif) {
+			/* No live interface owns this frame any more. Nothing from
+			 * here on may touch per-interface state, so drop the frame
+			 * without reporting a status for it.
+			 */
+			rcu_read_unlock();
+			MORSE_SKB_ERR_RATELIMITED(mors,
+				"tx finish with no live interface [chan:%u]\n",
+				tx_sts->channel);
+			mors->debug.page_stats.tx_status_dropped++;
+			morse_tx_complete_or_free(mors, skb, tx_sts->channel, false);
+			return 0;
+		}

--- a/rc.c
+++ b/rc.c
@@ -654,3 +654,4 @@

-	vif = txi->control.vif ? txi->control.vif : morse_get_vif_from_tx_status(mors, tx_sts);
+	vif = morse_skbq_tx_vif_get(mors, skb,
+				    MORSE_TX_CONF_FLAGS_VIF_ID_GET(le32_to_cpu(tx_sts->flags)));

@@ -662,2 +663,5 @@
 	if (!msta)
+		goto exit;
+
+	if (!vif)
 		goto exit;
--- a/bss_stats.c
+++ b/bss_stats.c
@@ -300,3 +300,3 @@

-	if (vif->type != NL80211_IFTYPE_AP || !skb ||
+	if (!vif || vif->type != NL80211_IFTYPE_AP || !skb ||
 		!morse_bss_stats_is_enabled(mors_vif))
--- a/bss_stats.h
+++ b/bss_stats.h
@@ -183,3 +183,4 @@
  *
- * @vif: pointer to ieee80211_vif
+ * @vif: pointer to ieee80211_vif. May be NULL when the interface a queued Tx
+ *       frame belonged to has already gone away, in which case this is a no-op.
  * @skb: Tx skbuff
--- a/pv1.c
+++ b/pv1.c
@@ -660,4 +660,4 @@
 {
-	struct morse_vif *mors_vif = ieee80211_vif_to_morse_vif(vif);
-	struct morse *mors = morse_vif_to_morse(mors_vif);
+	struct morse_vif *mors_vif;
+	struct morse *mors;
 	u16 pv1_fc = le16_to_cpu(pv1_hdr->frame_ctrl);
@@ -667,2 +667,8 @@
 	u16 aid;
+
+	if (!vif)
+		return NULL;
+
+	mors_vif = ieee80211_vif_to_morse_vif(vif);
+	mors = morse_vif_to_morse(mors_vif);

--- a/mac.c
+++ b/mac.c
@@ -3529,2 +3529,11 @@
 exit:
+	/* mac80211 does not call .remove_interface when .add_interface fails, so
+	 * the table entry has to be dropped here. Leaving it behind would outlive
+	 * the interface: the netdev is freed without .remove_interface ever being
+	 * called, and a queued Tx completion would then validate a stale cached
+	 * vif pointer against an equally stale table entry and accept it.
+	 */
+	if (ret && morse_get_vif_from_vif_id(mors, mors_vif->id) == vif)
+		morse_vif_remove(mors, mors_vif->id);
+
 	mutex_unlock(&mors->lock);
